# Code owners for the supply-chain surface: the files where a bad change is invisible in a
# normal diff review because it does not change any product behaviour.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES TODAY: GitHub auto-requests a review from the owner on any PR touching these
# paths. That is most useful for BOT pull requests (Dependabot, see .github/dependabot.yml) —
# GitHub never requests review from a PR's own author, so a human-authored PR is unaffected.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it is not a merge gate. CODEOWNERS only blocks merges when branch
# protection (or a ruleset) enables "Require review from Code Owners", and `main` is currently
# unprotected. That is deliberate, not an oversight: this repo has one maintainer, and a required
# review nobody else can give would deadlock every PR. Turn it on when a second maintainer exists.

# The dependency graph itself. A single edited line here can swap a package, and it will not show
# up as a behaviour change anywhere else in the diff.
/pnpm-lock.yaml           @sbuchweitz
/pnpm-workspace.yaml      @sbuchweitz
**/package.json           @sbuchweitz


# The gates. Weakening a check looks like a small refactor and silences the thing that would have
# caught the next problem — see the fail-open audit tolerance that shipped for months.
/scripts/verify.mjs       @sbuchweitz
/scripts/audit-gate.mjs   @sbuchweitz

# CI runs with credentials and can reach the registry, so a workflow edit is a privileged change.
/.github/                 @sbuchweitz
